"""
mbim_ims: the Windows counterpart of qmi_netdev.py. Brings up the IMS PDN of
an MBIM modem as a second bearer next to Windows' own internet connection,
finds its P-CSCF and serves the modem's SIM, all through Windows' MBIM stack
(no AT port, no driver changes).

    IMS PDN     an additional PDP context (Windows multi-PDP): Windows
                connects a second MBIM session on the IMS APN and gives it a
                child "Cellular N" adapter, up for as long as this runs
    P-CSCF      DHCPv6 Information-Request (RFC 3319 options), unicast to the
                IMS PDN's gateway, the alternative to the PCO that TS 24.229
                9.2.1 allows; Windows does not pass on the PCO's P-CSCF
    SIM         QMI UIM, carried by the modem's QMI-over-MBIM device service:
                APDUs go over a logical channel of our own (the modem keeps
                the basic one), AKA is an AUTHENTICATE on it

    python tools/mbim_ims.py                      # as Administrator
    python tools/mbim_ims.py --apn ims --listen 127.0.0.1:8888

The APN defaults to "apn" of the SIM's carrier bundle (by its IMSI), else
"ims". The SIM server has the API of simcard-server/server.py plus the
imei and msisdn extensions of serial_server.py, on --listen.

Windows routes the IMS adapter like any other, so it gets an IPv6 interface
metric of --metric (its default route must not take over from Wi-Fi), host
routes to the P-CSCFs, and the addresses Windows made up from the router
advertisement (SLAAC, temporary) are kept from being used as source, so SIP
and RTP leave from the address the network assigned. nekoims picks its SIP
and media address by the route to the P-CSCF, so nothing else is needed.

The P-CSCFs go to %ProgramData%\\NekoIMS\\pcscf, one per line, and the IMS
address to ims-address next to it (for nekoims -l: Windows would route the
P-CSCF from Wi-Fi's address otherwise), both rewritten when the IMS address
changes and removed while the PDN is down. When the PDN or
the modem goes away (suspend/resume) it is brought up again.

Tested with the Sierra Wireless EM7455 / Dell DW5811e (MBIM composition) on
Verizon. Needs Administrator (routes, address flags) and the pywinrt
packages:

    pip install winrt-Windows.Foundation winrt-Windows.Foundation.Collections \\
        winrt-Windows.Networking.Connectivity \\
        winrt-Windows.Networking.NetworkOperators winrt-Windows.Storage.Streams
"""
